    "q-113712": {
      "dom_id": "step-q113712",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int 9{x}^{8}(9+2{x}^{9})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "In this case, the term $9{x}^{8}$ outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $9+2{x}^{9}.$ But the derivative is $18{x}^{8},$ so we're only out by a constant factor.\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=9+2{x}^{9}.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =18{x}^{8}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=9{x}^{8}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9{x}^{8}(9+2{x}^{9})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (9+2{x}^{9})^{3}\\cdot 9{x}^{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{u}^{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(9+2{x}^{9})^{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{(9+2{x}^{9})^{4}}{8}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-113712-a",
